{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code-generating artificial intelligence agents has revolutionized software development, enabling automated code generation, debugging, and execution. However, these powerful capabilities introduce significant security vulnerabilities that require systematic evaluation. This paper presents a comprehensive overview of recent research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine the emerging landscape of automated red-teaming agents designed to probe code agents for vulnerabilities, including RedCodeAgent [1] and BlueCodeAgent [2]. Furthermore, we discuss the foundational transformer architecture [3] that powers these modern code agents. Our analysis reveals critical security challenges in code-executing AI systems and proposes directions for future research in secure code agent development and evaluation.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in software development, leveraging large language models (LLMs) to autonomously generate, modify, and execute code. These systems integrate with code interpreters and development environments, enabling dynamic execution, debugging, and interactive programming capabilities. The ability of code agents to perform complex workflows—from writing functions to deploying applications—has dramatically accelerated development cycles.\n\nHowever, the deployment of code agents introduces novel security challenges. Unlike traditional software, code agents can execute arbitrary code based on natural language instructions, creating attack vectors that traditional security measures cannot address. A malicious user might craft prompts designed to exploit vulnerabilities in code generation, leading to the production of malicious code, data exfiltration, or system compromise.\n\nRed-teaming—the process of systematically probing systems for vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating code agent security. Recent research has developed specialized red-teaming agents capable of automatically generating adversarial prompts and evaluating code agent responses for security issues. These tools enable systematic assessment of code agent robustness against various attack scenarios.\n\nThis paper surveys the current state of research in code agents and red-teaming, examining key contributions in the field and identifying open challenges for future work.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Code Agent Architectures\n\nThe foundation of modern code agents lies in the transformer architecture, which has become the dominant model for natural language understanding and generation. The transformer model, introduced in [3], employs self-attention mechanisms to capture long-range dependencies in input sequences without relying on recurrent or convolutional operations. This architecture has been adapted for code generation tasks, where attention mechanisms enable the model to attend to relevant code patterns and syntax during generation.\n\n### Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nRecent research has focused on developing automated red-teaming frameworks specifically designed to evaluate code agent security. One notable contribution is RedCodeAgent [1], an automatic red-teaming agent designed to probe diverse code agents for vulnerabilities. RedCodeAgent systematically generates adversarial prompts and evaluates code agent responses, identifying security weaknesses across multiple attack scenarios. The framework supports evaluation of various code agent architectures and execution environments, enabling comprehensive security assessment.\n\n### Blue Teaming for Code Agents\n\nComplementing red-teaming efforts, blue-teaming approaches focus on defensive strategies and secure code generation. BlueCodeAgent [2] presents a blue teaming agent enabled by automated red teaming for CodeGen AI systems. This work addresses the critical gap in defensive capabilities by developing agents that can both identify vulnerabilities through red-teaming and implement defensive measures to mitigate identified risks. The approach emphasizes the importance of adversarial training and continuous evaluation in maintaining code agent security.\n\n### Security Challenges in Code Agents\n\nCode agents face unique security challenges distinct from traditional software systems. These challenges include:\n\n1. **Prompt Injection**: Malicious prompts designed to manipulate code agent behavior\n2. **Code Execution Risks**: Risks arising from execution of generated code in sandboxed environments\n3. **Data Leakage**: Potential for code agents to inadvertently expose sensitive information\n4. **Adversarial Examples**: Code that appears benign but contains hidden malicious functionality\n\nThe papers surveyed in this section provide frameworks and methodologies for addressing these challenges through systematic evaluation and defensive mechanisms.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nResearch in code agents and red-teaming has made significant progress in understanding and addressing security challenges in code-generating AI systems. The development of specialized tools like RedCodeAgent [1] and BlueCodeAgent [2] represents important steps toward systematic evaluation of code agent security. Building on the transformer architecture [3], these tools enable automated identification of vulnerabilities and development of defensive strategies.\n\nFuture research directions should focus on: (1) developing more sophisticated attack methods that better simulate real-world threats, (2) creating standardized benchmarks for evaluating code agent security, (3) integrating security considerations into the design of code agents from the ground up, and (4) exploring the trade-offs between functionality and security in code agent systems. As code agents become increasingly prevalent in software development workflows, continued attention to security evaluation and improvement is essential for their safe and effective deployment.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[3] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n",
  "stderr": "",
  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat /app/paper.txt"
}'}

